{"request":{"path":"/rest/contents/search","query":{"limit":3,"offset":0,"projection":"full","sort":"viewsCount:desc"},"body":{"operator":"and","filters":{"format":{"includes":["video"]},"publishedAt":{"after":"2026-06-27","before":"2026-09-27"},"audioCopyrighted":true},"queries":[{"operator":"or","filters":{"caption":{"includesExactly":{"values":["running","runner","marathon"],"operator":"or"}}}}]}},"status":200,"fetchedAt":"2026-09-27T07:46:53.381Z","response":{"data":{"aggregations":{"totalViewsCount":1010440325,"totalInteractionsCount":47584000,"totalEngagementRatePerViews":0.0470923406585144,"totalEngagementRatePerFollowers":0.011988165405250944},"results":[{"id":"cnt_1c2e384e1a4d4b0bb99a74b53861","matchedQueries":[],"platform":"instagram","profileHandle":"inventionshark","profileDisplayName":"Invention Shark","format":"video","caption":"From above, train wheels look like they’re simply rolling along two steel rails. From underneath, you can see the engineering that actually keeps them there. Railway wheels aren’t shaped like ordinary car wheels. Their running surfaces are slightly tapered, while a raised flange sits along the inside edge of each wheel. Together with the fixed axle connecting the wheel pair, this geometry helps the train naturally stay centered as it moves down the track. The flange isn’t supposed to constantly grind against the rail. During normal operation, the wheel-and-rail geometry does much of the steering itself. The flange becomes particularly important when lateral movement gets large enough that additional guidance is needed. It’s a remarkably simple-looking system considering the enormous loads involved. Individual railcars can weigh tens of tons, yet their steel wheels maintain contact with rails across relatively small contact patches. And from a camera sitting just inches away, you get a completely different appreciation for how violent that interaction can look.","captionLanguage":"en","thumbnailMediaId":"med_23b23a34895c466f9529